  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors) and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers) are recognized as equal first-line options for managing primary hypertension .",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Both ACE inhibitors (angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors) and ARBs (angiotensin II receptor blockers) are recognized as equal first-line options for managing primary hypertension"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Comparison"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Efficacy: Both classes lower blood pressure and reduce cardiovascular risk with virtually identical effectiveness.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Mechanism of Action:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors (ending in -pril, e.g., lisinopril) stop the body from producing angiotensin II."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "ARBs (ending in -sartan, e.g., losartan, valsartan) block angiotensin II from binding to its target receptors.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  3,
                  4,
                  5
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects & Tolerability:",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "ACE inhibitors frequently cause a persistent dry cough (due to a buildup of bradykinin) and can rarely trigger angioedema ."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "ARBs block angiotensin II directly without affecting bradykinin, resulting in a much lower rate of dry cough and better overall patient adherence.",
                "reference_indexes": [
                  1,
                  5,
                  6
                ]
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              5,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Shared Precautions: Both can cause elevated potassium (hyperkalemia) and are strictly contraindicated during pregnancy .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Clinical Choice"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "While clinical guidelines treat them as interchangeable first-line agents, ARBs are frequently preferred over ACE inhibitors today because of their superior side effect profile, and they are standard alternatives for patients who cannot tolerate ACE inhibitor-induced cough. (Note: They should never be taken together).",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          6,
          7,
          8,
          9
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, tell me:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you comparing these for yourself or someone else ?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Are there any specific side effects or other health conditions (like kidney disease or diabetes) involved?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can provide more tailored information."
      }
